/* Elevate course catalogue */
const COURSES_A = [
{
  id:"c-proc-1", dom:"proc", title:"Strategic Sourcing Foundations",
  level:[1,2], cpd:9,
  blurb:"The full sourcing cycle from category analysis to award, built around how Canadian organizations actually run competitive processes.",
  modules:[
  { title:"Understanding the Category",
    lessons:[
    { id:"l1", title:"What sourcing is actually for", mins:6,
      body:"<p>Procurement gets described as buying things, which is the smallest part of the job. The function exists to convert organizational requirements into supply arrangements that deliver the required outcome at a defensible cost and an acceptable level of risk. Every one of those three words carries weight.</p><p><strong>Required outcome</strong> means the requirement has to be understood before it is priced. A specification written by the requisitioner without challenge frequently over-specifies, and over-specification is the single largest source of avoidable cost in most organizations.</p><p><strong>Defensible cost</strong> is not lowest price. It is a price you can explain: how it compares to the market, what it includes, and why the alternative was not chosen.</p><p><strong>Acceptable risk</strong> means supply continuity, compliance and reputational exposure are priced into the decision rather than discovered afterwards.</p><p>A sourcing professional who can hold all three at once is doing something a purchasing clerk is not, and the difference shows up in the job title and the salary band.</p>",
      keyPoints:["Requirements should be challenged before they are priced","Defensible cost means explainable, not lowest","Risk is part of the decision, not a separate exercise"],
      check:{q:"An engineer specifies a pump by manufacturer part number. What is the first sourcing question?",opts:["Which distributors carry that part","What performance the pump has to deliver, and whether the specification reflects a genuine requirement or habit","What the budget is","Who approved the requisition"],a:1,why:"Brand-specific specifications frequently encode habit rather than need. Establishing the functional requirement is what opens the category to competition."} },

    { id:"l2", title:"Building a spend baseline", mins:8,
      body:"<p>You cannot manage a category you have not measured. The spend baseline is the analytical foundation of everything that follows, and it is usually the first real piece of work on a new category.</p><p>A usable baseline answers four questions. <strong>Who are we buying from</strong>, consolidated properly so that the same supplier appearing under four name variants is counted once. <strong>What are we buying</strong>, grouped into categories that reflect how the market sells rather than how your general ledger codes. <strong>Who is buying it</strong>, by business unit and site. <strong>How is it being bought</strong>, meaning the share flowing through agreements versus one-off purchase orders.</p><p>That last question is where the surprises live. Maverick spend, the portion bought outside agreed channels, commonly runs between 15% and 40% in organizations that have never measured it, and it is usually the fastest value available because the agreements already exist.</p>",
      keyPoints:["Consolidate supplier name variants before analyzing","Group by how the market sells, not by GL code","Measure the share of spend flowing outside agreements"],
      check:{q:"Your ERP shows 140 suppliers in a category. After cleansing, 38 remain. What does this indicate?",opts:["The data extract was faulty","Duplicate and variant supplier records were inflating the count, which also means any prior analysis based on the raw list was unreliable","The category is too fragmented to manage","Suppliers have merged"],a:1,why:"Supplier master duplication is extremely common and silently corrupts spend analysis, which is why cleansing precedes conclusions."} },

    { id:"l3", title:"Segmenting with the Kraljic matrix", mins:7,
      body:"<p>Kraljic segments what you buy on two axes: profit impact, usually proxied by spend, and supply risk, meaning how hard the item would be to replace if supply failed. The four quadrants demand genuinely different strategies.</p><p><strong>Routine</strong> items are low on both. The goal is to reduce the cost of the transaction itself through catalogues, cards and automation, because the process costs more than the item.</p><p><strong>Leverage</strong> items are high spend, low risk. You have many capable suppliers and real money at stake, so consolidate volume and compete it hard.</p><p><strong>Bottleneck</strong> items are low spend, high risk. They can stop your operation but give you no commercial leverage. Secure continuity through dual sourcing, stocking agreements or buffer inventory.</p><p><strong>Strategic</strong> items are high on both. These warrant long-term relationships, joint planning and executive sponsorship, and there are usually far fewer of them than people assume.</p><p>The most common error is treating everything as leverage because tendering is the familiar tool.</p>",
      keyPoints:["Four quadrants require four different strategies","Bottleneck items need assurance of supply, not price pressure","Strategic relationships are expensive, so ration them"],
      check:{q:"A specialized calibration service costs $30,000 a year with one qualified provider in Western Canada. Which quadrant?",opts:["Routine","Leverage","Bottleneck","Strategic"],a:2,why:"Low spend with high supply risk is the bottleneck quadrant. The strategy is continuity, not competitive pressure."} }
  ]},
  { title:"Running the Process",
    lessons:[
    { id:"l4", title:"Choosing the right instrument: RFI, RFQ, RFP", mins:7,
      body:"<p>The choice follows from how well you can define what you need.</p><p>An <strong>RFI</strong> gathers market intelligence. Use it when you do not know who can do the work, what approaches exist, or what a realistic budget looks like. It is not a competitive process and should never be used to shortlist by stealth.</p><p>An <strong>RFQ</strong> compares price against a defined specification. Use it when you can say precisely what you want and the only meaningful difference between suppliers is commercial.</p><p>An <strong>RFP</strong> asks suppliers to propose how they would meet an outcome, and evaluates methodology, capability and price together. Use it when there is more than one credible way to solve the problem.</p><p>The mismatch that causes the most trouble is running an RFQ on a poorly defined requirement. Suppliers price different assumptions, the quotes are not comparable, and the apparent low bidder is simply the one who assumed the least scope.</p>",
      keyPoints:["Instrument follows requirement certainty","An RFI is intelligence gathering, not shortlisting","Unclear requirements make RFQ results incomparable"],
      check:{q:"You need a new warehouse management system but have not decided on the implementation approach. Which instrument?",opts:["RFQ, because the software is a defined product","RFP, because suppliers should propose approach, configuration and support model for evaluation alongside price","RFI only","Direct award to the ERP vendor"],a:1,why:"Where approach materially differs between suppliers and affects the outcome, it belongs in the evaluation. That is an RFP."} },

    { id:"l5", title:"Writing evaluation criteria that hold up", mins:8,
      body:"<p>Evaluation criteria are the promise you make to the market about how you will decide. Everything a proponent writes and prices is shaped by them, which is why changing them after submission is the most reliable way to lose a bid challenge.</p><p>Good criteria share four properties. They are <strong>published in advance</strong> with their weightings. They are <strong>measurable</strong>, meaning two competent evaluators reading the same proposal reach similar scores. They are <strong>relevant</strong> to the outcome rather than to supplier attributes that do not affect delivery. And they are <strong>complete</strong>, so nothing that will actually influence the decision sits outside the published set.</p><p>Build a scoring guide alongside the criteria that describes what a 3 looks like versus a 5. Without it, scores reflect evaluator temperament rather than proposal quality, and consensus meetings turn into negotiations between personalities.</p><p>Document a written rationale for every score. If the award is challenged, that record is your defence.</p>",
      keyPoints:["Publish criteria and weightings before submission","Write a scoring guide so scores mean the same thing to everyone","Document rationale for every score"],
      check:{q:"During evaluation you realize price was weighted too low. What can you do?",opts:["Adjust the weighting and re-score","Continue with the published weighting, and record the lesson for the next procurement","Cancel and reissue without explanation","Ask proponents to consent to a change"],a:1,why:"Proponents built their submissions around the published weighting. Changing it after the fact invalidates the basis on which they competed."} },

    { id:"l6", title:"Public procurement obligations in Canada", mins:9,
      body:"<p>If your organization is a public entity, or a broader public sector body such as a municipality, school board or health authority, your procurement is governed by trade agreements as well as your own policy.</p><p>The <strong>Canadian Free Trade Agreement</strong> requires open, non-discriminatory tendering above defined thresholds, posted on an electronic tendering system accessible across provinces. The <strong>New West Partnership Trade Agreement</strong> applies additionally across BC, Alberta, Saskatchewan and Manitoba, and in some areas reaches further than the CFTA. International agreements including CETA and CPTPP apply to listed entities above higher thresholds.</p><p>Three obligations cause most of the problems in practice. <strong>No local preference</strong>, which means an Alberta-first requirement is generally a breach. <strong>No artificial splitting</strong> of a requirement to fall below a threshold. And <strong>no criteria that favour incumbents</strong> without a genuine operational justification, such as requiring experience with your exact system.</p><p>Thresholds change on a two-year cycle, so verify the current figure rather than working from memory.</p>",
      keyPoints:["CFTA and NWPTA require open, non-discriminatory tendering above thresholds","Local preference and contract splitting are common breaches","Verify current thresholds rather than relying on memory"],
      check:{q:"A municipality splits a $180,000 requirement into three $60,000 purchases to stay under the tender threshold. This is:",opts:["Efficient procurement practice","A likely breach of trade agreement obligations, since splitting to avoid a threshold is specifically prohibited","Acceptable if the purchases are from different suppliers","Acceptable with council approval"],a:1,why:"Contract splitting to avoid open tendering obligations is expressly addressed in the trade agreements and is one of the most frequently challenged practices."} }
  ]},
  { title:"Award and Transition",
    lessons:[
    { id:"l7", title:"Debriefs and bid challenges", mins:6,
      body:"<p>A debrief is not a courtesy. Handled well it reduces challenge risk, improves the quality of future submissions from good suppliers, and protects your market's willingness to keep bidding.</p><p>The rule is simple: discuss the unsuccessful proponent's own submission, not anyone else's. Explain where their proposal scored well and where it fell short against the published criteria. Do not disclose competitors' pricing, scores or content, and do not compare them to the winner beyond what was published in the award notice.</p><p>A defensive or vague debrief reads as concealment and is one of the more common triggers for a formal challenge. Proponents who understand why they lost usually accept it; proponents who cannot tell what happened escalate.</p><p>Where a challenge does come, your evaluation record carries it. Scores with documented rationale, a consistent process, and criteria that never moved are what resolve it.</p>",
      keyPoints:["Discuss only the proponent's own submission","Vagueness invites challenges","The documented evaluation record is your defence"],
      check:{q:"An unsuccessful proponent asks what the winning price was. In a public procurement you should:",opts:["Refuse all pricing information","Provide the award value that is publicly disclosed under your policy and the applicable agreement, without disclosing competitors' detailed pricing","Provide the full bid tabulation","Refer them to legal"],a:1,why:"Award values are commonly published in public procurement, but detailed competitor pricing remains commercially confidential."} },

    { id:"l8", title:"Handing over to contract management", mins:6,
      body:"<p>The most expensive gap in procurement is the one between award and administration. The sourcing team holds the knowledge of what was promised, which assumptions underpinned the price, and where the risks sit. If that knowledge does not transfer, the contract is administered against the document alone and the value negotiated quietly evaporates.</p><p>A structured handover covers the obligations that need active monitoring, the service levels and how they will be measured, the key dates including renewal notice windows, the pricing mechanism and any indexation, and the assumptions that would justify reopening commercial terms if they prove wrong.</p><p>Run it as a meeting with the contract owner and the operational user, not as a forwarded folder. Fifteen minutes of context here routinely prevents a missed renewal or an unclaimed service credit worth far more.</p>",
      keyPoints:["Value is lost in the gap between award and administration","Transfer assumptions, not just documents","Renewal notice dates must land in someone's calendar"],
      check:{q:"The single most important item to transfer at handover is:",opts:["The full RFP file","The renewal notice date and who owns it, since a missed window removes your ability to re-compete","The supplier's contact list","The evaluation scores"],a:1,why:"Missed renewal windows silently remove leverage, and they are the most common and most costly handover failure."} },

    { id:"l9", title:"Measuring whether sourcing worked", mins:7,
      body:"<p>Procurement functions lose credibility through their own reporting more often than through their decisions. Savings claimed against a baseline finance does not recognize become a running argument, and the function is eventually treated as a processing centre.</p><p>Three principles keep the numbers trusted. <strong>Agree the baseline with finance before the sourcing event</strong>, not after the result is known. <strong>Separate savings from avoidance</strong>: savings reduce a budget line and finance can see them; avoidance prevents an increase and requires you to evidence a counterfactual. Both are legitimate, but reporting avoidance as budget-reducing saving destroys trust the first time it is tested.</p><p>Finally, <strong>report non-financial outcomes deliberately</strong>: risk reduced, continuity secured, compliance obligations met, emissions addressed. Categories that are managed for continuity rather than cost will never look good on a savings-only scorecard, and that scorecard will eventually push the function toward the wrong decisions.</p>",
      keyPoints:["Agree the baseline with finance before the event","Never report avoidance as budget-reducing saving","Savings-only reporting drives poor decisions on risk categories"],
      check:{q:"A supplier proposed a 9% increase; you settled at 3%. How should this be reported?",opts:["6% cost saving","6% cost avoidance, with the basis of the 9% proposal documented","3% cost increase only","Not reported"],a:1,why:"No budget line fell, so this is avoidance. Reporting it accurately, with evidence for the proposed increase, is what keeps the number credible."} }
  ]}]
},

{
  id:"c-proc-2", dom:"proc", title:"Advanced Category Management",
  level:[3,4], cpd:8,
  blurb:"Multi-year category strategy, supply market analysis and building leverage where the market does not hand it to you.",
  modules:[
  { title:"Reading the Supply Market",
    lessons:[
    { id:"l1", title:"Supply market analysis beyond the quote", mins:8,
      body:"<p>Category strategy starts with understanding the market you are buying in, which is a different exercise from understanding your own spend. You are looking for the structural facts that determine what is negotiable.</p><p><strong>Concentration</strong>: how many credible suppliers exist, and is the market consolidating? A market moving from eight players to three will reprice regardless of your skill.</p><p><strong>Capacity utilization</strong>: suppliers running at 95% behave differently from suppliers at 60%. The same negotiation produces opposite outcomes.</p><p><strong>Cost drivers</strong>: what actually moves supplier costs, and can you track those indices independently? This is what lets you distinguish a genuine increase from an opportunistic one.</p><p><strong>Your attractiveness as a customer</strong>: how do suppliers see your volume, payment behaviour, forecast reliability and ease of doing business? Organizations routinely negotiate as though they are a priority account when they are not, and the pricing reflects the supplier's view rather than their own.</p>",
      keyPoints:["Market structure determines what is negotiable","Track supplier cost indices independently","Your attractiveness as a customer shapes the price you are offered"],
      check:{q:"Two of five suppliers in your category merge. The most immediate implication is:",opts:["Prices will fall through scale efficiencies","Reduced competitive intensity, so existing agreements and their remaining terms should be reviewed before the market reprices","No material change","You should immediately tender"],a:1,why:"Consolidation reduces the number of credible alternatives, which weakens buyer leverage. The window to act is before renewal, not after."} },

    { id:"l2", title:"Building leverage where you have none", mins:8,
      body:"<p>Some categories hand you leverage: many suppliers, low switching cost, standard specification. The interesting work is in the categories that do not.</p><p>Four levers create leverage that the market did not give you. <strong>Aggregation</strong>, combining volume across sites, business units or, for associations and public bodies, across member organizations through cooperative arrangements. <strong>Specification change</strong>, opening a closed category by relaxing a requirement that only one supplier meets. <strong>Credible alternatives</strong>, qualifying a second source at small scale so the option becomes real rather than rhetorical. <strong>Demand management</strong>, reducing consumption, which is the only lever that works even in a genuine monopoly.</p><p>All four take months rather than weeks, which is why category strategy is planned on a multi-year horizon. Leverage that does not exist at the negotiation table has to have been built well before you sit down.</p>",
      keyPoints:["Aggregation, specification change, credible alternatives, demand management","Leverage is built months before the negotiation","Demand management works even against a monopoly"],
      check:{q:"A monopoly supplier of a regulated input raises prices annually above inflation. Which lever remains available?",opts:["Competitive tendering","Demand management: reducing consumption or substituting where the regulation permits","Switching to an overseas source","Refusing to pay"],a:1,why:"Where no alternative supply exists, the only remaining variable is how much you consume."} },

    { id:"l3", title:"The multi-year category plan", mins:7,
      body:"<p>A category plan is a document that tells someone taking over the category what is happening and why. If it cannot do that, it is a slide deck.</p><p>The useful structure is short. <strong>Current state</strong>: spend, suppliers, contracts and expiry dates, performance. <strong>Market</strong>: structure, direction, cost drivers. <strong>Risks</strong>: continuity, compliance, concentration, with a view on likelihood and impact. <strong>Strategy</strong>: what you intend to do over the next 24 to 36 months and why that approach rather than the alternatives. <strong>Roadmap</strong>: the sequenced actions with owners and dates, tied to contract expiry.</p><p>The section people skip is the alternatives considered and rejected. It is also the section that makes the plan defensible when a new executive asks why the category is not simply being tendered.</p>",
      keyPoints:["A category plan is a handover document, not a presentation","Sequence actions against contract expiry dates","Record the alternatives you rejected and why"],
      check:{q:"The most common failure in category plans is:",opts:["Insufficient market data","No sequenced roadmap tied to contract expiry, so the plan describes intent without ever being executed","Too much detail","Lack of executive sign-off"],a:1,why:"Strategy that is not anchored to specific dates and owners does not survive contact with day-to-day workload."} }
  ]},
  { title:"Supplier Portfolio and Risk",
    lessons:[
    { id:"l4", title:"Supply risk assessment that changes decisions", mins:7,
      body:"<p>Most supply risk registers are compliance artifacts. They list risks, score them on a matrix, and change nothing. A useful assessment is built to drive a specific decision.</p><p>Start from consequence rather than probability. Ask what would actually happen if this supplier stopped delivering tomorrow: how long until the operation is affected, what is the cost per week, and what is the realistic time to bring an alternative online. Those three numbers determine whether you need a second source, buffer stock, a stocking agreement or nothing at all.</p><p>Then look at concentration in less obvious places. Four suppliers who all depend on one sub-tier manufacturer are not four suppliers. Geographic concentration behaves the same way: three suppliers in one corridor share the same weather and the same closure.</p><p>Risk work earns its place when it changes what you buy, how much you hold, or who you qualify.</p>",
      keyPoints:["Start from consequence, not probability","Time to recover determines the mitigation","Look for hidden concentration at sub-tier and geographic level"],
      check:{q:"You have four suppliers for a component, all sourcing the same specialty alloy from one mill. Your effective supplier count is:",opts:["Four","One, for the risk that matters, because a mill disruption affects all of them simultaneously","Two","Cannot be determined"],a:1,why:"Sub-tier concentration removes the independence that makes multiple suppliers protective."} },

    { id:"l5", title:"Supplier performance management that works", mins:7,
      body:"<p>Scorecards fail in predictable ways. They measure what is easy to extract rather than what matters, they are sent rather than discussed, and they carry no consequence in either direction.</p><p>A scorecard that changes behaviour has four properties. It measures a <strong>small number of things that matter</strong>, typically delivery, quality, responsiveness and commercial compliance. The data is <strong>agreed as accurate</strong> before it is used, which usually means a calibration period where the supplier can dispute the figures. It is <strong>discussed in a forum</strong> with someone who can act on it, on both sides. And it is <strong>connected to something</strong>: more volume, longer term, joint improvement work, or a remediation requirement.</p><p>The most neglected part is the upside. Suppliers who consistently perform well and receive nothing for it eventually allocate their best capacity and their best people to customers who notice.</p>",
      keyPoints:["Measure few things, agree the data first","Discuss the scorecard rather than sending it","Connect performance to real consequence, including upside"],
      check:{q:"Your on-time delivery data shows 71% but the supplier disputes it, citing your late purchase orders. Best response?",opts:["Insist on your figure","Reconcile jointly, separating supplier-caused lateness from lateness caused by your own order timing, before the metric is used for any decision","Drop the metric","Escalate to their executive"],a:1,why:"A disputed metric drives no behaviour. Reconciliation often reveals that a meaningful share of the failure is internal, which is itself valuable."} },

    { id:"l6", title:"Knowing when to exit a supplier", mins:6,
      body:"<p>Exit decisions are delayed far longer than they should be, usually because the cost of switching is visible and immediate while the cost of staying is diffuse and ongoing.</p><p>Four signals justify a serious exit assessment. <strong>Performance has not responded to a structured remediation plan</strong>, meaning the problem is capability rather than attention. <strong>The commercial relationship has drifted materially out of market</strong> and will not correct. <strong>Risk has changed</strong>, through ownership change, financial distress or a compliance failure. Or <strong>your requirement has changed</strong> enough that the supplier is no longer a sensible fit.</p><p>Before committing, cost the exit honestly: transition effort, requalification, dual running, possible inventory build, and the internal time nobody budgets. Then compare it against the cost of another two years. Often the decision is obvious once both sides are actually written down, which is the argument for writing them down.</p>",
      keyPoints:["Cost of staying is diffuse and therefore underweighted","Remediation failure indicates capability, not attention","Write both costs down before deciding"],
      check:{q:"A supplier fails a remediation plan for the second time. The most important conclusion is:",opts:["The plan was poorly written","The gap is a capability limitation rather than a matter of focus, which changes the decision from management to replacement","More executive pressure is needed","Performance metrics should be relaxed"],a:1,why:"Repeated failure under a structured plan with clear targets indicates the supplier cannot do it, not that they have not tried."} }
  ]}]
},

{
  id:"c-cont-1", dom:"cont", title:"Contract Management Essentials",
  level:[1,3], cpd:10,
  blurb:"From drafting and risk allocation through to administration, claims and renewal, aimed at supply chain professionals rather than lawyers.",
  modules:[
  { title:"Structure and Risk",
    lessons:[
    { id:"l1", title:"How a commercial agreement is structured", mins:7,
      body:"<p>Most commercial agreements separate into three layers, and understanding the separation makes everything else easier.</p><p>The <strong>master agreement</strong> holds the terms that should not change from job to job: liability, indemnity, insurance, confidentiality, intellectual property, termination, governing law. It is negotiated once and carries forward.</p><p>The <strong>statement of work</strong> or schedule holds what is being delivered this time: scope, deliverables, acceptance criteria, schedule, and the pricing for that work.</p><p>The <strong>administrative layer</strong> holds what changes during performance: change orders, amendments, notices, approvals.</p><p>This structure is what allows an organization to add the fifth project with a supplier without reopening indemnity language. Where it goes wrong is when commercial terms leak into the SOW, or when a change order quietly modifies a master agreement term. Both happen routinely and both create disputes later, because nobody can say with confidence what the governing term actually is.</p>",
      keyPoints:["Master agreement for enduring terms, SOW for the specific work","The structure lets you add work without renegotiating legal terms","Watch for change orders that quietly alter master terms"],
      check:{q:"A change order adds scope and also extends the limitation of liability cap. The concern is:",opts:["Change orders cannot add scope","A master agreement term is being modified through an administrative instrument, often without the review that term originally received","The cap should never change","Nothing, this is routine"],a:1,why:"Terms that received legal review at the master level should not be altered through a document processed as routine administration."} },

    { id:"l2", title:"Allocating risk: the clauses that matter", mins:9,
      body:"<p>Four clauses carry most of the commercial risk, and supply chain professionals should be able to read them without waiting for legal.</p><p><strong>Limitation of liability</strong> caps exposure. The question is not whether there is a cap but whether it is proportionate to the loss a failure could actually cause. A cap at contract value is meaningless when the contract is $50,000 and the failure stops a $400,000-per-week operation.</p><p><strong>Indemnity</strong> shifts responsibility for defined third-party claims. Read the trigger: an indemnity limited to gross negligence or wilful misconduct excludes ordinary negligence, which causes most real losses.</p><p><strong>Force majeure</strong> excuses performance for defined events. Check what is listed, whether it suspends or terminates, and whether it excuses payment as well as delivery.</p><p><strong>Termination</strong> defines your exit. Termination for convenience is valuable and usually carries a cost; termination for cause needs a workable cure mechanism rather than one that cannot practically be exercised.</p>",
      keyPoints:["Test whether the liability cap is proportionate to real exposure","Indemnity triggers set at gross negligence exclude most actual losses","Check whether force majeure suspends or terminates"],
      check:{q:"A $60,000 maintenance contract caps liability at contract value. The equipment failure risk is $350,000 per incident. The issue is:",opts:["The cap is standard and acceptable","The cap leaves the overwhelming majority of the realistic loss with you, so it should be negotiated against the risk rather than the contract value","Liability caps are unenforceable","Insurance makes the cap irrelevant"],a:1,why:"Caps benchmarked to contract value rather than exposure are common and frequently leave the buyer carrying risk they did not price."} },

    { id:"l3", title:"Service levels that produce performance", mins:7,
      body:"<p>A service level has three parts: the measure, the target, and the consequence. Weakness in any one makes the whole thing decorative.</p><p>The <strong>measure</strong> must be objectively determinable from data both parties accept. Response time measured from when a ticket was logged is workable; measured from when the supplier became aware is not.</p><p>The <strong>target</strong> should reflect what the business needs rather than what the supplier comfortably achieves. Targets set at current performance guarantee no improvement.</p><p>The <strong>consequence</strong> is where most agreements are weakest. Service credits compensate for failure but do not compel improvement, and a supplier may rationally treat them as a cost of doing business. Mature agreements ladder it: credits first, then a mandatory remediation plan once a threshold is crossed, then a termination right for chronic failure.</p><p>Without that ladder you can be four months into sustained underperformance with no contractual route out.</p>",
      keyPoints:["Measures must come from data both parties accept","Targets set at current performance guarantee no improvement","Ladder consequences: credits, remediation, termination right"],
      check:{q:"A supplier consistently pays service credits rather than improving. This indicates:",opts:["The credits are too small","The agreement lacks an escalating consequence, so paying is a rational commercial choice for the supplier","The supplier is acting in bad faith","Service levels do not work"],a:1,why:"If paying is cheaper than fixing, a commercially rational supplier pays. The design fault is the absence of escalation."} }
  ]},
  { title:"Administration",
    lessons:[
    { id:"l4", title:"The contract register", mins:6,
      body:"<p>Organizations lose more value to administrative invisibility than to poor negotiation. Contracts auto-renew because a date passed. Service credits go unclaimed because nobody tracked the failures. Price indexation is applied incorrectly because nobody checked the formula.</p><p>The fix is unglamorous. A contract register capturing counterparty, value, start and end dates, renewal notice date, notice method, owner, and key obligations gives you a portfolio view that almost no organization has by default.</p><p>Build it before buying a contract lifecycle management platform. A platform implemented over an unknown portfolio digitizes the confusion, and you cannot write sensible requirements for the tool until you know what it has to hold. The register is cheap, prevents the most expensive failures immediately, and becomes your migration data set later.</p><p>The single highest-value field is the renewal notice date with an owner and a calendar alert well ahead of it.</p>",
      keyPoints:["Most value is lost to administrative invisibility","Build the register before buying the platform","The renewal notice date is the highest-value field"],
      check:{q:"Before procuring a CLM platform, the most valuable preparatory step is:",opts:["Selecting a vendor shortlist","Building a contract register so the portfolio is visible and the tool's requirements can be specified from evidence","Standardizing templates","Training staff on the new system"],a:1,why:"You cannot specify or configure a system sensibly for a portfolio you have not yet mapped."} },

    { id:"l5", title:"Change orders and scope creep", mins:7,
      body:"<p>Scope creep rarely arrives as a single decision. It accumulates through small accommodations, each reasonable on its own, none documented, until the delivered scope no longer matches what was priced and nobody can point to when it changed.</p><p>The discipline is to treat every scope question as a scope question first and a relationship question second. When a supplier raises a change, compare the claimed work against the SOW and the agreed baseline. If it genuinely sits outside, it is a legitimate change and should be priced and documented. If it does not, the documented comparison is what lets you say so without it becoming personal.</p><p>The reverse matters as much. Work your own team asks for informally, outside the SOW, is a change too, and suppliers who absorb a series of those will eventually recover the cost somewhere less visible.</p><p>Verbal agreements followed by written confirmation are workable. Verbal agreements alone are how disputes start.</p>",
      keyPoints:["Creep accumulates through undocumented small accommodations","Compare claimed work against the SOW baseline first","Informal requests from your own team are changes too"],
      check:{q:"Your operations lead informally asks the supplier for extra reporting each month. The right response is:",opts:["Nothing, it is minor","Document it as a change, priced or formally absorbed, so the scope of record matches what is actually being delivered","Instruct the supplier to refuse","Reduce the contract price accordingly"],a:1,why:"Undocumented additions create a gap between the contract and reality that surfaces at renewal or in a dispute."} },

    { id:"l6", title:"Managing renewals and re-competition", mins:7,
      body:"<p>A renewal decision should begin six to twelve months before expiry, not in the notice window. By the time the window opens, your realistic options have usually already narrowed to renewing.</p><p>Work backwards from the expiry date. If re-competition would take four months and transition another three, the decision point is at least eight months out. Anything later and renewal becomes the only executable option regardless of what the analysis says.</p><p>The renewal assessment asks four things: has the supplier performed, is the commercial arrangement still competitive, has our requirement changed, and what has happened in the supply market. Answering them requires the performance data and market intelligence to have been gathered during the term rather than assembled hurriedly at the end.</p><p>Where you do renew, renew deliberately. An evergreen rollover is not a decision, and a supplier can generally tell the difference between a customer who chose them and one who ran out of time.</p>",
      keyPoints:["Work backwards from expiry to find the real decision date","Four questions: performance, commercial, requirement, market","An evergreen rollover is not a decision"],
      check:{q:"A contract expires in 90 days. Re-competition takes four months. Your position is:",opts:["Run an expedited tender","Renewal is effectively the only executable option, so negotiate from that reality and fix the timeline for the next cycle","Extend month to month indefinitely","Terminate and source urgently"],a:1,why:"Recognizing that leverage has already been lost leads to better handling than pretending an option exists. The lesson is calendar discipline for next time."} }
  ]}]
},

{
  id:"c-nego-1", dom:"nego", title:"Commercial Negotiation for Supply Chain",
  level:[1,4], cpd:9,
  blurb:"Preparation, value creation and supplier relationship governance, including what to do when you hold no leverage at all.",
  modules:[
  { title:"Preparation",
    lessons:[
    { id:"l1", title:"BATNA, reservation price and the zone of agreement", mins:7,
      body:"<p>Three concepts do most of the work in negotiation theory, and confusing them is the most common preparation failure.</p><p>Your <strong>BATNA</strong> is what you do if there is no agreement. Not your worst acceptable deal, but the actual alternative course of action. It is the source of your leverage, and it exists whether or not you have identified it.</p><p>Your <strong>reservation price</strong> is the point past which your BATNA is better than the deal. It follows from the BATNA rather than from a target.</p><p>The <strong>ZOPA</strong> is the range between your reservation point and theirs. If it is empty, no amount of skill produces agreement, and recognizing that early saves everyone time.</p><p>The practical consequence is that the highest-value preparation work is usually spent improving your BATNA rather than rehearsing arguments. A buyer with a qualified alternative negotiates differently from one without, and the supplier can tell.</p>",
      keyPoints:["BATNA is an alternative course of action, not a price","Reservation price follows from BATNA","Improving your BATNA beats rehearsing arguments"],
      check:{q:"You have no alternative supplier and cannot delay. Your strongest preparation move is:",opts:["Setting an ambitious target price","Working on the variables other than price, and beginning to build an alternative for the next cycle, while being realistic about this one","Concealing your urgency","Opening with an aggressive anchor"],a:1,why:"Without a BATNA, price leverage is limited. Value is found in terms, and the structural fix belongs to the next cycle."} },

    { id:"l2", title:"Mapping tradeable variables", mins:7,
      body:"<p>Negotiations reduce to a single-variable fight over price when the parties have not listed what else is on the table. Before any commercial discussion, write out every variable that has different value to each side.</p><p>Typical candidates: contract term, volume commitment, payment terms, delivery schedule and lead time, forecast sharing, minimum order quantity, packaging and customization, warranty period, service level targets, liability allocation, exclusivity, and who holds inventory.</p><p>The value of the list is that these items are rarely valued equally. Longer term may be worth a great deal to a supplier planning capacity and very little to you. Forecast visibility may cost you almost nothing and materially reduce their risk. Each asymmetry is a trade that can create value rather than merely divide it.</p><p>Bring the list into the room. Under time pressure, negotiators default to the one number everyone understands, and the list is what pulls the conversation back out.</p>",
      keyPoints:["List every variable before discussing price","Value asymmetries are where joint value is created","Time pressure collapses negotiations to price"],
      check:{q:"A supplier will not move on unit price. Which trade most likely funds a reduction?",opts:["Asking again with a deadline","Offering a longer term with a volume commitment, which lets them plan capacity and reduces their own risk","Threatening to tender","Requesting a smaller order"],a:1,why:"Commitments that reduce the supplier's cost or risk let them fund a lower price without losing margin."} },

    { id:"l3", title:"Understanding the other side's position", mins:7,
      body:"<p>Preparation that focuses only on your own position produces a negotiator who is well prepared to talk and poorly prepared to trade.</p><p>Ask four questions about the supplier. <strong>What are their alternatives</strong>, meaning how much do they need this business? A supplier at capacity is a different counterpart from one with idle plant. <strong>What pressures are they under</strong>, such as quarter end, a capacity gap, a reference they want, or an input cost they are absorbing. <strong>Who is actually deciding</strong>, and does the person in the room have authority? <strong>How do they make money on this</strong>, meaning where does their margin actually sit, which is often in service, parts or renewals rather than the item you are discussing.</p><p>That last point changes strategy frequently. Pressing hard on a line the supplier does not earn on may produce a concession that is recovered immediately in an area you did not look at.</p>",
      keyPoints:["Assess their alternatives and pressures, not just yours","Identify where their margin actually sits","Concessions on low-margin lines are often recovered elsewhere"],
      check:{q:"An equipment supplier concedes readily on machine price but holds firm on service rates. This most likely indicates:",opts:["Machine price is negotiable and service is not","Their margin sits in the service and parts stream, so total cost of ownership rather than purchase price is the negotiation that matters","They are being unreasonable","Service rates are regulated"],a:1,why:"Recognizing where the profit lives moves the negotiation to where the money actually is."} }
  ]},
  { title:"At the Table and After",
    lessons:[
    { id:"l4", title:"Anchoring, concessions and the shape of a negotiation", mins:8,
      body:"<p>First offers anchor. The effect is well established and it operates even on experienced negotiators who know about it. The practical implications are to prepare your own anchor with a rationale, and to have a method for defusing theirs.</p><p>Defusing an anchor is not counter-anchoring at an equally extreme number, which frequently stalls the conversation. It is asking how the figure was constructed. Cost breakdowns move the discussion from position to reasoning, and reasoning can be examined.</p><p>Concession patterns communicate as much as the numbers. Large, fast, unreciprocated concessions signal that more will follow, which invites the other side to wait. Concessions should shrink in size, be given in exchange for something, and be explained.</p><p>Watch your own deadline behaviour. Most avoidable concessions in commercial negotiation are made in the final hours by the side with the harder deadline, which is why concealing urgency and building in genuine time are worth more than any tactic used in the room.</p>",
      keyPoints:["Defuse anchors by asking how they were built","Concessions should shrink and be exchanged","Deadline pressure causes most avoidable concessions"],
      check:{q:"You concede 4%, then 4% again, quickly and without asking for anything. The supplier most likely concludes:",opts:["You are negotiating in good faith","More is available if they hold their position, since the pattern shows no resistance","You have reached your limit","The negotiation is over"],a:1,why:"Unreciprocated concessions of constant size signal further room and rationally encourage waiting."} },

    { id:"l5", title:"Negotiating from weakness", mins:7,
      body:"<p>Sometimes you have no alternative, the supplier knows it, and the textbook advice does not apply. This situation is common and worth handling deliberately rather than pretending otherwise.</p><p>Three things remain available. <strong>Time</strong>: a short bridging arrangement rarely costs much and converts an immediate ultimatum into a process you can work within. <strong>Verification</strong>: if the demand is justified by input costs, ask for the basis and check it against published indices, because opportunistic increases often do not survive that request. <strong>The future</strong>: the supplier is making a decision about a relationship, not just a transaction, and how this is handled affects what happens when you do have options.</p><p>What does not work is bluffing about alternatives that do not exist. Suppliers generally know their market position better than you know it, and a bluff that is called costs you credibility in every subsequent conversation.</p><p>Meanwhile, start building the alternative. The real fix belongs to the next cycle.</p>",
      keyPoints:["Buy time with a bridge rather than accepting an ultimatum","Ask for the basis of a cost-driven increase","Do not bluff about alternatives that do not exist"],
      check:{q:"A sole-source supplier demands 20% with 30 days' notice. Your first move is:",opts:["Refuse and invoke the contract","Agree to protect supply","Propose a short bridging arrangement while verifying the cost claim and starting to qualify an alternative","Escalate to litigation"],a:2,why:"A bridge protects the operation, verification tests the claim, and qualification changes your position for the next conversation."} },

    { id:"l6", title:"Governing the relationship after signature", mins:7,
      body:"<p>The value negotiated into an agreement is realized or lost during the term, and a governance structure is what determines which.</p><p>Effective governance is proportionate. For most suppliers it means a defined contract owner, a performance measure that is actually reviewed, and a route for raising issues. For strategic suppliers it means a tiered structure: operational contact for day-to-day, a management review on a regular cadence covering performance and issues, and an executive relationship that exists before it is needed.</p><p>That last point is the one organizations get wrong. An executive relationship established during a crisis is a request for help from a stranger. Established beforehand, it is a call to someone with a reason to care.</p><p>Keep the quarterly review forward-looking. A meeting that only reviews last quarter's numbers becomes a ritual. One that covers upcoming demand, capacity, risk and improvement opportunities is worth both parties' time and usually surfaces the problems early.</p>",
      keyPoints:["Governance should be proportionate to supplier importance","Build executive relationships before you need them","Keep reviews forward-looking or they become ritual"],
      check:{q:"A strategic supplier's quarterly reviews have become a recitation of past metrics. The fix is:",opts:["Cancel the reviews","Restructure the agenda around forward-looking items: demand outlook, capacity, risks and improvement opportunities, with metrics circulated in advance","Escalate to executives","Increase review frequency"],a:1,why:"Metrics can be read beforehand. The meeting's value is in the discussion of what is coming."} }
  ]}]
}
];
